 {"id":"DF-OFF-BY-ONE-58","title":"[P3] `seed` discards the corpus's own created_at: CorpusFile/CORPUSAnswer parse the field (internal/seed/seed.go:35,43) but store inserts omit it (internal/graph/store.go:231,345), so every seeded row is stamped with the seed run's clock - 2856 classes carry 60 distinct timestamps across the 59s seed, and the corpus dates never reach the graph or /api/v1/problems","status":"complete","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"type":"task","created_by":"dogfood-off-by-one-2026-10-23-1243","created_at":"2026-10-23T12:56:53Z","updated_at":"2026-11-21T12:10:20Z","capability_tags":["seed","data-integrity","provenance"],"reasoning":"Evidence from the run's own database: corpus file data/answers/0112-serde-json-validator-jsonschema.json declares created_at 2026-07-25 01:10:08 and its two answers carry 2026-07-25 01:10:08 and 2026-10-01 07:20:47. After `off-by-one seed` (release v0.2.0) the served rows read created_at 2026-10-23T12:52:11Z (GET /api/v1/problems?q=json) and the graph row is problem_classes(id=89, created_at '2026-10-23 12:52:03'). Census: `select count(distinct created_at), count(*) from problem_classes` = 60 | 2856, all values inside 2026-10-23 12:52:0x..12:52:5x - i.e. the seed run's clock, not the corpus's history. Mechanism: internal/seed/seed.go:35 and :43 declare CreatedAt on both structs and nothing reads it; internal/graph/store.go:231 inserts `problem_classes (title, description)` and :345 inserts `answer_nodes (class_id, parent_id, env, lang, version, solution, evidence, signatures, status)`, so both rows fall back to the schema DEFAULT (datetime('now')) at sql/schema/schema.sql:8,22,31. User impact: 'what was added since my last sync' is unanswerable on a fresh node, a seeded catalog is indistinguishable from one solved minutes ago, and provenance of a pre-verified answer is lost (the ranking/ordering paths use id DESC so nothing else breaks).","acceptance_criteria":"Fresh DB seeded from the bundled corpus (./off-by-one seed